Code change observation
- Frame implementation: two listener registration lines added.
- Named inner class: create one handler object and register it twice.
- Anonymous class: store the listener in a variable and register it twice.
- Lambda: create one lambda variable and register it twice.
The conversion logic does not need to be duplicated.
Task 5.3 – Compare the Styles (2 Marks)
Comparison table
Feature	Frame	Named inner	Anonymous inner	Lambda
Readability	Moderate	Good	Good for short logic	Very good
Access to frame fields	Direct	Direct	Direct	Direct
Handler reuse	Reuse frame	Easy	Possible	Easy
Code length	Moderate	Longer	Longer	Short
Maintenance	Moderate	Good	Moderate	Good
Separate handler class	No	Yes	No	No

Explanation
1. Frame implements ActionListener
- The main frame handles the event.
- Easy access to all frame fields.
- Can make the class large when many buttons are used.
2. Named inner class
- The event-handling code has a separate name.
- Can be reused for multiple components.
- Suitable when event-handling logic is large.
3. Anonymous inner class
- Creates a listener directly where it is required.
- Useful for small event-handling operations.
- Reuse is less convenient unless the listener is stored in a variable.
4. Lambda expression
- Requires less code.
- Easy to understand for simple event handling.
- Suitable for small operations and multiple event registrations.
Recommended
 For a large application: I would use named handler classes or Swing Action objects because they separate event-handling logic from the GUI and support reuse. For simple applications, lambda expressions are easier and shorter.
Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Method reference
Instead of writing:
convertButton.addActionListener(e -> convert());


We can write:
convertButton.addActionListener(this::convert);


The method reference directly calls the convert() method when the event occurs.

For both triggers:
ActionListener handler = this::convert;

convertButton.addActionListener(handler);
amount.addActionListener(handler);


2. Shared Swing Action
A Swing Action can be reused by a button, menu item and keyboard shortcut.
Action convertAction = new AbstractAction("Convert") {

    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
        convert();
    }
};

convertButton.setAction(convertAction);

JMenuItem menuItem = new JMenuItem(convertAction);


A keyboard shortcut can also be registered using InputMap and ActionMap.
3. Comparison
Feature	Four event styles	Method reference	Shared Swing Action
Listener objects	Usually one per handler registration strategy	One shared listener	One shared Action
Reuse	Depends on implementation	Easy	Very easy
New button trigger	Register handler	Register handler	Attach same Action
Menu item	Additional registration	Additional registration	Direct reuse
Keyboard shortcut	Additional registration	Additional registration	ActionMap and InputMap
Maintenance	Depends on style	Simple	Best for shared commands

4. Time and space complexity
Let k represent the number of event sources.

Time complexity:
- Each event is dispatched to its registered listener.
- Event dispatch takes O(1) time with respect to the number of triggers.
- Adding one new trigger requires O(1) registration work.
- Registering the same handler with k components takes O(k) total setup time.
Space complexity:
- Separate listener objects: O(k) in the worst case.
- One shared listener: O(1) listener storage, plus O(k) component registrations.
- One shared Swing Action: O(1) Action object, plus O(k) component references or registrations.
Conclusion: Method references reduce code for calling an existing method. A shared Swing Action is more suitable when the same command must work from buttons, menu items and keyboard shortcuts.

Final Result
The currency converter was implemented using four event-handling styles: frame implementation, named inner class, anonymous inner class and lambda expression. The application was modified to support both button clicks and Enter-key events. It displays the converted amount to two decimal places and handles invalid input using a dialog box. Lambda expressions are suitable for simple applications, while named handlers and shared Swing Actions are useful for larger applications.
